orresponding Fourier series of $f(x)$ must be zero." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054024/state.json" "file_sha256" "a578368f64098a0adbf164936377dd1cba580901958410399146891d40907a88"} {"question" "q-340797" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To check whether $f$ is even or odd, we compare $f(-x)$ and $f(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-x)-(-x)^{2} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)-{x}^{2} \\\\  & =f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(-x)=f(x),$ we have that $f$ is even.\n\nFor the Fourier series on $[-\\pi ,\\pi ],$ the sine coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nHere, $f(x)$ is even and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)$ is odd, so $f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)$ is odd.\n\nNow, the integral of an odd function over $[-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ is $0.$ Thus, ${b}_{n}=0$ for all $n\\ge 1.$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ is $\\text{even}$ and the $\\text{sine terms}$ in its Fourier series must be zero." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054024/state.json" "file_sha256" "a578368f64098a0adbf164936377dd1cba580901958410399146891d40907a88"} {"question" "q-340797" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054024/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7aa216e64ce4c7359463a76d1167acab42f18802fbfbb34ddb4e1322e354f254"} {"question" "q-340797" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054024/state.json" "file_sha256" "a578368f64098a0adbf164936377dd1cba580901958410399146891d40907a88"} {"question" "q-340797" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "even"] ["text" "odd"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054024/state.json" "file_sha256" "a578368f64098a0adbf164936377dd1cba580901958410399146891d40907a88"} {"question" "q-340797" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "even"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054024/state.json" "file_sha256" "a578368f64098a0adbf164936377dd1cba580901958410399146891d40907a88"} {"question" "q-340797" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054024/state.json" "file_sha256" "a578368f64098a0adbf164936377dd1cba580901958410399146891d40907a88"} {"question" "q-340797" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "cosine terms"] ["text" "sine terms"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054024/state.json" "file_sha256" "a578368f64098a0adbf164936377dd1cba580901958410399146891d40907a88"} {"question" "q-340797" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "sine terms"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054024/state.json" "file_sha256" "a578368f64098a0adbf164936377dd1cba580901958410399146891d40907a88"} {"question" "e-21803" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx) \\\\ g(x) & ∼\\frac{{c}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{c}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{d}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ be $2\\pi$-periodic extensions of some functions from $x \\in [-\\pi,\\pi)$ to $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$ with the Fourier series shown above.  What are the Fourier constant coefficient, the $3\\textrm{rd}$ Fourier sine coefficient, and the $8\\textrm{th}$ Fourier cosine coefficient of $-3f(x)+4g(x)$?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054024/example-21803.json" "file_sha256" "71e46013d37a5bf45a88c946549a8c1430f63e10bc144ba1261b22c0143ac50f"} {"question" "e-21803" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ be $2\\pi$-periodic functions with Fourier series\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)), \\\\ g(x) & ∼\\frac{{c}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({c}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{d}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen for any constants $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$,\n\n$$\n\\alpha f(x) + \\beta g(x)\n\\sim\n\\frac{\\alpha a_0 + \\beta c_0}{2}\n+\n\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\n\\Big(\n(\\alpha a_n + \\beta c_n)\\cos(nx)\n+\n(\\alpha b_n + \\beta d_n)\\sin(nx)\n\\Big).\n$$\n\nUsing the linearity property of Fourier series, the corresponding Fourier coefficients of $-3f(x)+4g(x)$ are\n\n- $-3a_0+4c_0$ for the constant coefficient,\n\n- $-3a_n+4c_n,$ $n \\ge 1$ for the cosine coefficients, and\n\n- $-3b_n+4d_n,$ $n \\ge 1$ for the sine coefficients.\n\nTherefore,\n\n- the Fourier constant coefficient of $-3f(x)+4g(x)$ is $-3a_0+4c_0$,\n\n- the $3$rd Fourier sine coefficient of $-3f(x)+4g(x)$ is $-3b_3+4d_3$, and\n\n- the $8$th Fourier cosine coefficient of $-3f(x)+4g(x)$ is $-3a_8+4c_8$." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054024/example-21803.json" "file_sha256" "71e46013d37a5bf45a88c946549a8c1430f63e10bc144ba1261b22c0143ac50f"} {"question" "e-21805" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054024/assets/238174bfcd0e07603a8516f9271efff876e36cf8021403be57b049ec31b60977.png)\nLet ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054024/assets/a600da93d54ed8b4857befa58c1165e8c02b77d1c2fc5b1f460961dc135c50a7.png) be the ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054024/assets/1898332940848e9192873afe50ec9d9a005baf8bcb86ce77c66231321ba3fe0e.png)-periodic extension of a function from ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054024/assets/5ff1e4e25630b889a7d37b5ac7252742b9b0f0abdf659ece1f3b0700815ddc44.png) to ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054024/assets/d33b55bb712e2f8437446299936998a79ee357a6aa77ce6c31932bb464868bb8.png) with the Fourier series shown above. What is the ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054024/assets/db700c8ee08453d9f4b0f347c4f88daf4186495494840905e9196b2243899653.png)th sine Fourier coefficient of ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054024/assets/c38b7489bfb362de743d5a53941eaddfc382f5d50756b13bc6a4fda893a692e9.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054024/example-21805.json" "file_sha256" "e2e5b4a9cdbd159eeb6e879e1f9eb792ce0f5d97b23e8954661998cb30dd1df0"} {"question" "e-21805" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The Fourier series of ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054024/assets/9363d37d53374519f1170d2c1c24ecb690a6810a304207ef21a0d1f3eba609de.png) is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054024/assets/51c0039997e64ade9fb16a68ccaf87323ba205a85deb1020b5f74cd072dbf8f2.png)\n\nRecall that the corresponding Fourier coefficients of ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054024/assets/5c09997f1e5ab45e800dfc2a1fdbd766ce0e96033dd656a3128a5543bb6894a5.png) are\n\n- ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054024/assets/fdd27d69b84de936d93ebc06bc7e813d9002f40141d1d79fa787e46fc3dc4d23.png) for the constant coefficient,\n\n- ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054024/assets/6bd1cc889fa6814efed8288ad3a4be7c23fd86cd74c239ee933f770c87eb756d.png) ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054024/assets/f582369f4b0075444e7e450e4a2b92413b8f5d55be2f27222637c1eb56cddd0f.png) for the cosine coefficients, and\n\n- ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054024/assets/230850b2bfeae1c1cf561825fb543c43e08e105076be3f1241f5e4590f4bf537.png) ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054024/assets/bbfce07e48b9a1eb79082aa046d29029cb6771b8243b5af1c0b9a353a5f1e379.png) for the sine coefficients.\n\nTherefore, the ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054024/assets/16cfd7f94cefe4ac0dca3045d59624a00c7f962d2a2cc1138a2407db521c8800.png)th sine coefficient of ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054024/assets/55bae3cc544df2d070b283a68deef7a5e53cf0f456aabd8062d0346a52662c03.png) is ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054024/assets/9264ca9ad64ff750e95f9523e56c79e665af874d3b13d601018061499c64ec41.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054024/example-21805.json" "file_sha256" "e2e5b4a9cdbd159eeb6e879e1f9eb792ce0f5d97b23e8954661998cb30dd1df0"} {"question" "e-21804" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x)=x\\cos(2x)$ on $x \\in [-\\pi,\\pi].$ Complete the statements below by filling in the blanks.\nDetermine if the function $f(x)$ is odd or even, and which terms of the corresponding Fourier series of $f(x)$ must be zero." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054024/example-21804.json" "file_sha256" "008ceb8ba48d76c2cfe6667d0d43b289ebac9b2a2bf7eeb987bcebfa1433e476"} {"question" "e-21804" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To determine whether $f$ is even or odd, we compare $f(-x)$ and $f(x){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(-x)) \\\\  & =(-x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-2x) \\\\  & =(-x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) \\\\  & =-f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(-x) = -f(x),$ we have that $f$ is odd.\n\nFor Fourier series on $[-\\pi,\\pi],$ the cosine coefficients are given by\n\n$$\na_n=\\frac{1}{\\pi}\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi} f(x)\\cos(nx)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nand the constant coefficient is\n\n$$\na_0=\\frac{1}{\\pi}\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi} f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nHere, $f(x)$ is odd and $\\cos(nx)$ is even, so $f(x)\\cos(nx)$ is odd. Also, $f(x)$ itself is odd.\n\nNow, the integral of an odd function over $[-\\pi,\\pi]$ is $0.$ Thus, $a_n=0$ for all $n\\ge1,$ and $a_0=0.$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ is $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\text{odd}}$ and the $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\text{cosine}}$ terms  in its Fourier series must be zero." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14054024/example-21804.json" "file_sha256" "008ceb8ba48d76c2cfe6667d0d43b289ebac9b2a2bf7eeb987bcebfa1433e476"}] "usage" {"q-338682" [] "e-21804" [] "e-21805" [] "e-21803" []} "basis" 2234 "retractions" [[17592186229676 :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x) = x\\cos (x)$ on $x ∈ [- π, π]$. Select the correct options below.\nThe function $f(x)$ is\n{{field-1}}.\n\nConsequently, the\n{{field-2}}\nin the corresponding Fourier series of $f(x)$ must be zero."] [17592186228891 :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}f(x) & ∼\\frac{a_{0}}{2} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)a_{n}\\cos (nx) + b_{n}\\sin (nx) \\\\ g(x) & ∼\\frac{c_{0}}{2} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)c_{n}\\cos (nx) + d_{n}\\sin (nx)\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nLet $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ be $2\\pi$-periodic extensions of some functions from $[MATH: x \\in [-\\pi,\\pi)]$ to $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$ with the Fourier series shown above. What are the Fourier constant coefficient, the $3\\textrm{rd}$ Fourier sine coefficient, and the $8\\textrm{th}$ Fourier cosine coefficient of $-3f(x)+4g(x)$?"] [17592186228891 :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ be $2\\pi$-periodic functions with Fourier series\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}f(x) & ∼\\frac{a_{0}}{2} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)(a_{n}\\cos (nx) + b_{n}\\sin (nx)), \\\\ g(x) & ∼\\frac{c_{0}}{2} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)(c_{n}\\cos (nx) + d_{n}\\sin (nx))\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen for any constants $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$,\n\n$$\n\\alpha f(x) + \\beta g(x) \\sim \\frac{\\alpha a_0 + \\beta c_0}{2} + \\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} \\Big((\\alpha a_n + \\beta c_n)\\cos(nx) + (\\alpha b_n + \\beta d_n)\\sin(nx) \\Big)\n$$\n\nUsing the linearity property of Fourier series, the corresponding Fourier coefficients of $-3f(x)+4g(x)$ are\n\n- $-3a_0+4c_0$ for the constant coefficient,\n- $-3a_n+4c_n$, $n \\ge 1$ for the cosine coefficients, and\n- $-3b_n+4d_n$, $n \\ge 1$ for the sine coefficients.\n\nTherefore,\n\n- the Fourier constant coefficient of $-3f(x)+4g(x)$ is $-3a_0+4c_0$,\n- the $3$rd Fourier sine coefficient of $-3f(x)+4g(x)$ is $-3b_3+4d_3$, and\n- the $8$th Fourier cosine coefficient of $-3f(x)+4g(x)$ is $-3a_8+4c_8$."] [17592186228893 :question/problem "$f(x) \\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}a_n\\cos(nx)+b_n\\sin(nx)$\nLet $f(x)$ be the $2\\pi$-periodic extesion of a function from $[MATH: x \\in [-\\pi,\\pi)]$ to $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$ with the Fourier series shown above. What is the $5$th sine Fourier coefficient of $f(-x)$?"] [17592186228893 :question/worked-solution "The Fourier series of $f(x)$ is\n\n$$\nf(x) \\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}a_n\\cos(nx)+b_n\\sin(nx)\n$$\n\nRecall that the corresponding Fourier coefficients of $f(-x)$ are\n\n- $a_0$ for the constant coefficient,\n- $a_n$ $n \\ge 1$, for the cosine coefficients, and\n- $-b_n$ $n \\ge 1$, for the sine coefficients.\n\nTherefore, the $5$th sine coefficient of $f(-x)$ is $-b_5$."] [17592186228892 :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=x\\cos(2x)$ on $x \\in [-\\pi,\\pi]$. Complete the statements below by filling in the blanks.\nDetermine if the function $f(x)$ is odd or even, and which terms of the corresponding Fourier series of $f(x)$ must be zero."] [17592186228892 :question/worked-solution "To determine whether $f$ is even or odd, we compare $f(-x)$ and $f(x){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(-x) &= (-x)\\cos (2(-x)) \\\\\n&= (-x)\\cos (-2x) \\\\\n&= (-x)\\cos (2x) \\\\\n&=-f(x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\nf(-x) = -f(x)\n$$\n\nwe have that $f$ is odd.\n\nFor Fourier series on $[-\\pi,\\pi]$, the cosine coefficients are given by\n\n$$\na_n=\\frac{1}{\\pi}\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi} f(x)\\cos(nx)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nand the constant coefficient is\n\n$$\na_0=\\frac{1}{\\pi}\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi} f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nHere, $f(x)$ is odd and $\\cos(nx)$ is even, so $f(x)\\cos(nx)$ is odd. Also, $f(x)$ itself is odd.\n\nNow, the integral of an odd function over $[-\\pi,\\pi]$ is $0$. Thus, $a_n=0$ for all\n\n$$\nn\\ge1\n$$\n\nand $a_0=0$.\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ is $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\text{odd}}$ and the $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\text{cosine}}$ terms in its Fourier series must be zero."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186228507 17592186228508 17592186228510 17592186228511] "immutable_fields" [[17592186228506 :answer-field/correct] [17592186228506 :answer-field/id] [17592186228506 :answer-field/key] [17592186228506 :answer-field/type] [17592186228509 :answer-field/correct] [17592186228509 :answer-field/id] [17592186228509 :answer-field/key] [17592186228509 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
